# Copy to .env (git-ignored) and adjust.

# The LAN interface every published port binds to. The hub has NO auth —
# pin this to your server's LAN IP so nothing else can ever reach it.
# Losing this line is not a small thing: unset, every port falls back to
# 0.0.0.0 and the hub answers on every interface the box has.
HUB_BIND=192.168.1.50

# Local timezone: all "today" / chore-day math uses this.
TZ=America/Los_Angeles

# The uid:gid the hub runs as — must match the owner of ./data
# (run `id -u` and `id -g`; the defaults suit the first user on most distros).
HUB_UID=1000
HUB_GID=1000

# iCloud CalDAV (optional). A DEDICATED bot Apple ID + an app-specific password
# (appleid.apple.com -> Sign-In and Security -> App-Specific Passwords), added as
# an EDITOR sharee on the family calendars/reminders. Leave unset to keep the
# CalDAV integration off (it stays hidden in the settings menu). A revoked or
# expired password is handled gracefully: the wall shows "reconnect" and keeps
# serving the cached view. Secrets — never commit .env.
# ICLOUD_CALDAV_USER=bot-appleid@icloud.com
# ICLOUD_CALDAV_APP_PASSWORD=xxxx-xxxx-xxxx-xxxx

# Home Assistant long-lived access token for the laundry card (optional;
# pairs with the `laundry` block in config.json). Create a DEDICATED token
# (HA -> your profile -> Security -> Long-lived access tokens) so it's
# revocable without touching anything else. Leave unset to keep the laundry
# integration off. Secrets — never commit .env.
#
# This file exists ONLY on your deploy box: no git copy, anywhere. Compose
# reads it at `up` time, so if a deploy or a stray `--delete` removes it, the
# next container starts with an empty token: the wall's laundry card goes to
# "unavailable", the settings row says "reconnect", and the log says so once at
# startup. Back it up, or exclude it from whatever syncs the box.
# HA_TOKEN=eyJhbGciOi...

# Serve HTTPS instead of HTTP (optional; both empty = plain HTTP, today's
# only behavior). Needed for the Google reconnect flow below, and for
# nothing else — the wall's own LAN/Tailscale use doesn't need this on its
# own. Paths are INSIDE the container: point these at wherever you bind-mount
# the cert/key from, e.g. put them in ./data/tls/ on the box and set these to
# /data/tls/hub.crt / /data/tls/hub.key. A Tailscale cert
# (`tailscale cert <name>.<tailnet>.ts.net`) is the easy way to get a real,
# trusted cert with no domain of your own — see README.
# TLS_CERT_FILE=/data/tls/hub.crt
# TLS_KEY_FILE=/data/tls/hub.key

# Wall-triggered Google Calendar reconnect (optional). Without these three,
# the ONLY way to (re)connect Google Calendar is running scripts/google-auth.py
# on a desktop and copying token.json to the box — that keeps working exactly
# as before regardless of whether you set these up. With them, Settings gets
# a real "Reconnect Google Calendar" button: tap it, approve on Google's page,
# token.json is written on the box, no desktop needed.
#
# Setup (once):
#  1. In the SAME Google Cloud project scripts/google-auth.py already uses,
#     create a SECOND OAuth client — type "Web application", not "Desktop app".
#  2. Its "Authorized redirect URIs" must contain EXACTLY the URL you put in
#     GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI below (scheme, host, port, path, no trailing
#     slash difference — Google matches it byte-for-byte).
#  3. That redirect URI must be HTTPS with a real hostname — Google rejects a
#     plain http:// URL, and rejects a raw IP address outright (LAN or
#     Tailscale IP both fail). If you're using Tailscale, `tailscale cert
#     <your-box>.<tailnet>.ts.net` mints a real, publicly-trusted cert for
#     free, scoped to your private tailnet — see TLS_CERT_FILE/TLS_KEY_FILE
#     above and README. Put the resulting files somewhere under ./data (e.g.
#     ./data/tls/) so the container can read them.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=xxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=GOCSPX-xxxxx
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_REDIRECT_URI=https://your-box.your-tailnet.ts.net:8138/oauth/google/callback
